
<% content_for :title, "Reflections | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="perspectives-reflections-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="perspectives-reflections-hero">

    <div class="perspectives-reflections-hero-content">

      <p class="perspectives-reflections-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / PERSPECTIVES / REFLECTIONS
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>SHIT</span>
        <span>I'VE BEEN</span>
        <span>THINKING</span>
        <span>ABOUT.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="perspectives-reflections-intro">
        THINGS YOU'VE LEARNED.<br>
        THINGS YOU'VE QUESTIONED.<br>
        THINGS YOU HAVEN'T FIGURED OUT YET.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- INTRO -->
  <section class="pr-section pr-section-black">

    <p class="pr-number">01 / REFLECTIONS</p>

    <h2>
      I DON'T HAVE<br>
      A DAMN<br>
      THESIS.
    </h2>

    <div class="pr-copy">

      <p>
        You spend a considerable amount of time
        trying to understand yourself. Why you
        made certain decisions, why you reacted
        the way you did, what you believe now,
        and whether you'd do things differently
        if you had another chance.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes you come up with an explanation
        that makes sense. Other times you realize
        you've been telling yourself the same
        story for years without questioning
        whether it's entirely accurate.
      </p>

      <p>
        You've had experiences that changed
        how you understand yourself, other
        people, and the world. Some were
        consequences of your own decisions.
        Others were circumstances you
        couldn't control.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't need to turn all of them
        into lessons or find something
        meaningful in every unpleasant
        thing that's happened.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes you're just interested
        in a question. Sometimes you
        reconsider something you used
        to believe. Sometimes you realize
        you've been wrong about a
        subject you were previously
        very confident about.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's worth writing down.
      </p>

      <p>
        These aren't final conclusions.
        They're things you can return
        to, disagree with, expand on,
        or completely change your
        mind about later.
      </p>

      <div class="pr-callout">
        YOU'RE ALLOWED TO LEARN SOMETHING,<br>
        CHANGE YOUR MIND, AND REALIZE<br>
        YOU STILL DON'T HAVE THE WHOLE ANSWER.
      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- REFLECTIONS -->
  <section class="pr-reflections">

    <div class="pr-reflections-heading">

      <p>02 / THINGS ON YOUR MIND</p>

      <h2>
        THINKING<br>
        OUT<br>
        LOUD.
      </h2>

      <p>
        Things worth considering without
        needing to turn them into conclusions.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="pr-grid">


      <!-- REFLECTION 01 -->
      <article class="pr-card pr-card-pink">

        <p class="pr-card-number">01</p>

        <p class="pr-card-topic">
          RECOVERY
        </p>

        <h3>
          WHAT DOES<br>
          PROGRESS<br>
          EVEN MEAN?
        </h3>

        <p>
          It's easy to measure recovery
          by the things you haven't done.
          You haven't been drinking.
          You haven't repeated certain
          mistakes. You've avoided
          situations that used to
          cause problems.
        </p>

        <p>
          Those things matter, but they
          don't tell you everything
          about how you're changing.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can stop a behavior
          without understanding it.
          You can also understand a
          behavior perfectly well
          and still struggle
          to change it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Maybe progress isn't always
          visible in the outcome.
          Sometimes it's in what
          you notice, what you
          question, or the decisions
          you make differently.
        </p>

        <div class="pr-card-question">

          <span>WHAT YOU'RE ASKING</span>

          <p>
            Are you measuring your progress
            by how your life actually changes,
            or by how well you're following
            an idea of what recovery
            is supposed to look like?
          </p>

        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- REFLECTION 02 -->
      <article class="pr-card pr-card-yellow">

        <p class="pr-card-number">02</p>

        <p class="pr-card-topic">
          IDENTITY
        </p>

        <h3>
          WHO ARE<br>
          YOU WITHOUT<br>
          THE CHAOS?
        </h3>

        <p>
          When instability has been part
          of your life for a long time,
          it's difficult to imagine
          yourself without it.
        </p>

        <p>
          You've experienced intense
          moods, impulsive decisions,
          drinking, unexpected consequences,
          and periods when life seemed
          to change faster than you
          could make sense of it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Those experiences influenced
          your identity, but they
          didn't create your entire
          personality.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can enjoy excitement,
          spontaneity, and a little
          unpredictability without
          needing your life to
          become a disaster.
        </p>

        <p>
          Maybe stability doesn't
          require you to become
          less interesting. Maybe
          you're still discovering
          what your interests
          look like without
          everything else
          competing for attention.
        </p>

        <div class="pr-card-question">

          <span>WHAT YOU'RE ASKING</span>

          <p>
            Which parts of your old life
            do you genuinely miss,
            and which parts have simply
            become so familiar that
            you mistake them for
            who you are?
          </p>

        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- REFLECTION 03 -->
      <article class="pr-card pr-card-purple">

        <p class="pr-card-number">03</p>

        <p class="pr-card-topic">
          CONSEQUENCES
        </p>

        <h3>
          UNDERSTANDING<br>
          ISN'T THE SAME<br>
          AS EXCUSING.
        </h3>

        <p>
          You can explain why you
          made a decision without
          pretending it was acceptable.
        </p>

        <p>
          Maybe you were manic.
          Maybe you were intoxicated.
          Maybe you were in pain,
          angry, frightened, or
          desperate for something
          to feel different.
        </p>

        <p>
          Those circumstances matter.
          They help explain how you
          arrived at a decision.
          They don't automatically
          erase what happened
          afterward.
        </p>

        <p>
          But responsibility doesn't
          require you to deny the
          circumstances either.
          You can examine what
          happened without constructing
          an elaborate defense
          or pretending you're
          completely terrible.
        </p>

        <p>
          The uncomfortable part
          is accepting that you
          might understand exactly
          why you did something
          and still have to
          live with the consequences.
        </p>

        <div class="pr-card-question">

          <span>WHAT YOU'RE ASKING</span>

          <p>
            Can you tell the whole
            truth about what happened
            without using the explanation
            to excuse yourself or
            the consequences to
            define yourself?
          </p>

        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- REFLECTION 04 -->
      <article class="pr-card pr-card-black">

        <p class="pr-card-number">04</p>

        <p class="pr-card-topic">
          BIPOLAR / IDENTITY
        </p>

        <h3>
          WHICH VERSION<br>
          OF YOU IS<br>
          THE REAL ONE?
        </h3>

        <p>
          You've experienced versions
          of yourself that seem almost
          incompatible with one another.
        </p>

        <p>
          One version is confident,
          energetic, full of ideas,
          and convinced that anything
          is possible. Another can
          barely imagine enjoying
          anything again.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then there's the person
          looking back at both
          experiences, trying to
          understand what happened
          and which thoughts
          belonged to whom.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's tempting to decide
          that one version is
          your real personality
          and the others are
          simply your illness.
        </p>

        <p>
          But your experiences
          aren't always that
          easy to separate.
          You can recognize
          that your mood affected
          your behavior without
          pretending the person
          experiencing it
          wasn't you.
        </p>

        <div class="pr-card-question">

          <span>WHAT YOU'RE ASKING</span>

          <p>
            If your feelings, convictions,
            and understanding of yourself
            can change so dramatically,
            what actually makes you
            the same person
            through all of it?
          </p>

        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- REFLECTION 05 -->
      <article class="pr-card pr-card-yellow">

        <p class="pr-card-number">05</p>

        <p class="pr-card-topic">
          ADDICTION
        </p>

        <h3>
          YOU DIDN'T<br>
          DRINK FOR<br>
          NO REASON.
        </h3>

        <p>
          Alcohol wasn't appealing
          simply because it was
          available. You liked
          what it did for you.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes it made you
          happy when you were sad.
          Sometimes it made pain
          or anxiety easier to
          tolerate. Sometimes
          you wanted excitement,
          pleasure, or a break
          from your own thoughts.
        </p>

        <p>
          And sometimes you just
          liked being drunk.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need to pretend
          those experiences weren't
          enjoyable to understand
          why drinking became
          such a problem.
        </p>

        <p>
          But removing alcohol
          doesn't automatically
          remove the feelings,
          circumstances, or habits
          you associated with it.
        </p>

        <p>
          You still have to
          live with yourself,
          including on the
          days when that
          isn't particularly fun.
        </p>

        <div class="pr-card-question">

          <span>WHAT YOU'RE ASKING</span>

          <p>
            When you miss drinking,
            are you missing the
            alcohol itself, what
            it made you feel,
            or the opportunity
            to stop feeling
            something else?
          </p>

        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- REFLECTION 06 -->
      <article class="pr-card pr-card-pink">

        <p class="pr-card-number">06</p>

        <p class="pr-card-topic">
          CONTROL
        </p>

        <h3>
          WHY DO YOU<br>
          NEED EVERYONE<br>
          TO UNDERSTAND?
        </h3>

        <p>
          You can explain yourself
          carefully, provide every
          relevant detail, and still
          have someone misunderstand
          what you're saying.
        </p>

        <p>
          They might disagree with
          your reasoning. They might
          think you were wrong.
          They might simply have
          a different interpretation
          of what happened.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes their criticism
          is unfair. Sometimes
          they're noticing something
          you don't want to admit.
        </p>

        <p>
          But you can't make
          another person understand
          something by explaining
          it indefinitely.
        </p>

        <p>
          At some point, you're
          no longer providing
          information. You're
          trying to control
          the conclusion.
        </p>

        <p>
          And other people are
          allowed to reach
          conclusions you
          don't particularly like.
        </p>

        <div class="pr-card-question">

          <span>WHAT YOU'RE ASKING</span>

          <p>
            Are you trying to
            communicate something
            important, or are you
            trying to make another
            person agree with
            your version of it?
          </p>

        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- REFLECTION 07 -->
      <article class="pr-card pr-card-purple">

        <p class="pr-card-number">07</p>

        <p class="pr-card-topic">
          SPIRITUALITY
        </p>

        <h3>
          MAYBE YOU<br>
          DON'T NEED<br>
          ONE ANSWER.
        </h3>

        <p>
          You're interested in
          different spiritual
          traditions because
          they offer different
          ways of understanding
          experiences that
          aren't easily explained.
        </p>

        <p>
          Buddhism asks you to
          examine suffering and
          attachment. Hinduism
          offers ideas about
          consciousness, action,
          and the nature of self.
          Taoism invites you to
          consider what happens
          when you stop trying
          to force everything
          into place.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need to
          make every tradition
          agree with every other
          one before finding
          something meaningful
          in its teachings.
        </p>

        <p>
          And you don't need
          a definitive explanation
          for every spiritual
          experience or belief.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes you can
          remain curious without
          turning curiosity
          into a demand
          for certainty.
        </p>

        <div class="pr-card-question">

          <span>WHAT YOU'RE ASKING</span>

          <p>
            What becomes possible
            when you stop trying
            to determine which
            perspective is the
            final answer and
            start considering
            what each one
            helps you notice?
          </p>

        </div>

      </article>


      <!-- REFLECTION 08 -->
      <article class="pr-card pr-card-black">

        <p class="pr-card-number">08</p>

        <p class="pr-card-topic">
          STARTING OVER
        </p>

        <h3>
          HOW MANY<br>
          TIMES CAN YOU<br>
          START OVER?
        </h3>

        <p>
          Starting over sounds
          romantic until you're
          the one who has
          to actually do it.
        </p>

        <p>
          You have to deal
          with the practical
          consequences, explain
          your circumstances,
          rebuild routines,
          and occasionally accept
          that things you once
          took for granted
          aren't available anymore.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's frustrating to
          compare your present
          circumstances with
          where you expected
          to be by now.
        </p>

        <p>
          But the comparison
          doesn't change
          what needs to
          happen next.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to
          describe every new
          beginning as an
          exciting opportunity.
          Sometimes it's simply
          what your circumstances
          require.
        </p>

        <p>
          And sometimes what
          you build afterward
          turns out to be
          something you
          wouldn't have
          considered before.
        </p>

        <div class="pr-card-question">

          <span>WHAT YOU'RE ASKING</span>

          <p>
            What would you do
            with your current
            circumstances if
            you stopped measuring
            them against the
            life you thought
            you should already have?
          </p>

        </div>

      </article>


    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- CONTRADICTIONS -->
  <section class="pr-section pr-section-yellow">

    <p class="pr-number">03 / CONTRADICTIONS</p>

    <h2>
      BOTH CAN<br>
      BE TRUE.
    </h2>

    <div class="pr-copy">

      <p>
        You're increasingly aware
        that two things can be true
        without canceling each
        other out.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can understand why
        you did something and
        still recognize that
        it was wrong. You can
        miss an experience and
        still have no intention
        of repeating it.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't have to
        choose one feeling,
        explanation, or perspective
        and pretend everything
        else is irrelevant.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some contradictions
        are worth examining
        rather than trying
        to resolve.
      </p>

      <div class="pr-both-grid">

        <div>
          <span>YOU CAN BE</span>
          <strong>GRATEFUL</strong>
          <p>
            for parts of your life
            and still be angry
            about what happened.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>YOU CAN TAKE</span>
          <strong>RESPONSIBILITY</strong>
          <p>
            without deciding your
            mistakes are the only
            important things about you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>YOU CAN HAVE</span>
          <strong>COMPASSION</strong>
          <p>
            for someone and still
            recognize that their
            behavior is unacceptable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>YOU CAN BE</span>
          <strong>STRUGGLING</strong>
          <p>
            with something you've
            dealt with before without
            pretending you've learned
            absolutely nothing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>YOU CAN</span>
          <strong>FORGIVE</strong>
          <p>
            someone without wanting
            them back in your life.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>YOU CAN</span>
          <strong>CHANGE</strong>
          <p>
            without rewriting the
            past to make yourself
            look better.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        Accepting a contradiction
        doesn't mean refusing
        to make judgments.
        It means allowing
        yourself to consider
        more than one truth
        before reaching them.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- QUESTIONS -->
  <section class="pr-section pr-section-purple">

    <p class="pr-number">04 / QUESTIONS</p>

    <h2>
      SHIT I DON'T<br>
      KNOW THE<br>
      ANSWER TO.
    </h2>

    <div class="pr-copy">

      <p>
        You've spent plenty of
        time searching for explanations.
        Sometimes understanding
        something genuinely helps.
        Other times you keep
        examining the same question
        because you're uncomfortable
        not having an answer.
      </p>

      <p>
        Maybe you don't need
        to resolve every thought.
        Some questions can
        remain interesting
        without becoming
        problems to solve.
      </p>

      <p>
        And some answers might
        change depending on
        when you ask.
      </p>

      <div class="pr-questions">

        <p>
          Who are you when
          you're not reacting
          to something?
        </p>

        <p>
          What are you still
          trying to prove,
          and to whom?
        </p>

        <p>
          Which parts of
          your personality
          do you mistake
          for symptoms?
        </p>

        <p>
          What does enough
          actually look like
          when you stop
          comparing yourself
          to other people?
        </p>

        <p>
          What are you
          holding onto
          because it's familiar,
          rather than because
          you still want it?
        </p>

        <p>
          Which stories about
          yourself have you
          repeated so often
          that you've stopped
          questioning them?
        </p>

        <p>
          What do you
          genuinely enjoy
          when you aren't
          trying to accomplish
          anything?
        </p>

        <p>
          How much of your
          desire for certainty
          comes from being
          uncomfortable with
          not knowing?
        </p>

        <p>
          What would you
          do differently
          if you weren't
          worried about
          explaining yourself?
        </p>

        <p>
          Can you appreciate
          an experience without
          wanting it back?
        </p>

        <p>
          What have you
          changed your mind
          about that once
          seemed absolutely
          obvious?
        </p>

        <p>
          What might you
          be wrong about
          right now?
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- CHANGING YOUR MIND -->
  <section class="pr-section pr-section-pink">

    <p class="pr-number">05 / CHANGING YOUR MIND</p>

    <h2>
      OLD ME<br>
      DOESN'T GET<br>
      A VETO.
    </h2>

    <div class="pr-copy">

      <p>
        You've believed things
        you no longer believe.
        You've defended choices
        you wouldn't make now.
        You've been convinced
        you understood a situation
        and later discovered
        you were missing
        something important.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's uncomfortable,
        especially when you
        remember how certain
        you were at the time.
      </p>

      <p>
        But having believed
        something doesn't mean
        you have to keep
        defending it indefinitely.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can reconsider an
        opinion without pretending
        you never held it.
        You can acknowledge that
        you misunderstood someone
        without making up an
        excuse for it.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can even change
        your mind about something
        that was once an important
        part of your identity.
      </p>

      <p>
        That doesn't necessarily
        mean the old version
        of you was dishonest.
        Maybe you believed what
        you believed because
        of the information,
        experiences, and perspective
        you had at the time.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now you have different
        information. You have
        different experiences.
        You might also have
        a different perspective.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can do something
        with that instead
        of defending an
        outdated conclusion.
      </p>

      <div class="pr-callout">
        IF NEW INFORMATION CAN'T CHANGE<br>
        YOUR MIND, ARE YOU ACTUALLY<br>
        THINKING — OR JUST DEFENDING?
      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- WRITING -->
  <section class="pr-section pr-section-black">

    <p class="pr-number">06 / WRITE IT DOWN</p>

    <h2>
      GET IT OUT<br>
      OF YOUR<br>
      DAMN HEAD.
    </h2>

    <div class="pr-copy">

      <p>
        You're capable of having
        a conversation with yourself
        in which you present
        the argument, defend
        your position, challenge
        the opposition, and
        declare yourself the winner.
      </p>

      <p>
        It's remarkably convenient
        when you're responsible
        for every side of
        the discussion.
      </p>

      <p>
        Writing makes that
        process a little less 
        convenient. Once an
        idea is on the page,
        you can actually
        look at what
        you've said.
      </p>

      <p>
        You might notice
        an assumption you
        hadn't questioned,
        a contradiction you
        overlooked, or an
        explanation that sounded
        much more convincing
        in your head.
      </p>

      <p>
        You also preserve
        something about who
        you were when you
        wrote it.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's part of
        what makes journals
        interesting. You can
        return to an entry
        months or years later
        and encounter a version
        of yourself who saw
        the world differently.
      </p>

      <p>
        Maybe you agree with
        that person. Maybe you
        don't. Maybe you barely
        recognize the reasoning.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't have
        to correct every
        old entry or
        rewrite the story.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes it's worth
        leaving the original
        thought exactly where
        it is and considering
        how your understanding
        has changed.
      </p>

      <div class="pr-thought">
        SOMETIMES YOUR THOUGHTS SOUND<br>
        A LOT LESS CONVINCING ONCE<br>
        THEY'RE SITTING ON PAPER.
      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="pr-ending">

    <p>
      NOTICE IT.<br>
      QUESTION IT.<br>
      WRITE IT DOWN.
    </p>

    <h2>
      KEEP<br>
      THINKING.
    </h2>

    <p class="pr-ending-copy">
      You don't need to finish every thought.
      Some of them are worth keeping
      precisely because you haven't
      figured them out.
    </p>

    <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
        perspectives_path,
        class: "pr-back-link" %>

  </section>

</main>
